(function(){
  const P = window.TWOFY_PEDIA = window.TWOFY_PEDIA || {};
  function set(key, f) { P[key] = Object.assign(P[key] || {}, f); }
  set("lm:liberty", {extract:"The Statue of Liberty stands on Liberty Island in New York Harbor, a copper figure dedicated in 1886 as a gift from France. Bartholdi designed the statue; the iron frame inside was the work of Gustave Eiffel. From the air it is a small green figure in a wide harbor of bridges, ferries, and the towers of Lower Manhattan. The copper skin is only a few millimeters thick and has weathered to the familiar green.", fact:"Emma Lazarus’s sonnet was added to the pedestal years later, after the statue had already become a symbol of arrival."});
  set("lm:empire", {extract:"The Empire State Building rises from Midtown Manhattan as a limestone Art Deco tower completed in 1931. It was built in a little over a year during the Depression and held the title of world’s tallest building for about four decades. From above, the slender mast and stepped crown are easy to pick out among the surrounding grid. Lights on the top still change color for civic occasions.", fact:"The mast was designed so airships could dock there. None ever did."});
  set("lm:wtc", {extract:"One World Trade Center stands at the northwest corner of the rebuilt World Trade Center site in Lower Manhattan. The spire reaches 1,776 feet, a height chosen to match the year of the Declaration of Independence, which makes it the tallest building in the Western Hemisphere. The square shaft sits on an octagonal plan and turns as it rises, so the glass catches light differently through the day. The memorial pools of the original twin towers lie immediately south.", fact:"Construction finished in 2013, and tenants began moving in the following year."});
  set("lm:golden", {extract:"The Golden Gate Bridge crosses the strait between San Francisco and Marin County, a suspension span opened in 1937 and painted International Orange so it would show in fog. From the air the two towers sit in a tidal gate famous for wind, with the Pacific on one side and the bay on the other. The main cables hold tens of thousands of wires each. It is still a working highway, not a museum piece.", fact:"A safety net hung under the roadway during construction saved a number of workers who would otherwise have fallen."});
  set("lm:alcatraz", {extract:"Alcatraz is a rocky island in San Francisco Bay, used as a lighthouse, a military post, and then a federal penitentiary from 1934 to 1963. The prison closed because it cost too much to supply on an island in a cold current. From the air it is a small citadel in the middle of a huge bay, with San Francisco’s skyline close across the water. Native activists occupied the island from 1969 to 1971.", fact:"No confirmed escapees are known to have survived the swim, though a 1962 attempt is still argued over."});
  set("lm:hollywood", {extract:"The Hollywood Sign sits on Mount Lee above Los Angeles, white block letters first put up in 1923 as HOLLYWOODLAND and shortened in 1949. Each letter is about 45 feet tall. It began as a real-estate advertisement and outlived the housing tract it was meant to sell. From the air the letters are a small, bright line on a brushy ridge above a vast basin of streets and hills.", fact:"A 1978 restoration replaced the crumbling original letters with the ones standing now."});
  set("lm:needle", {extract:"The Space Needle was built for the 1962 World’s Fair in Seattle, a tripod tower with a saucer on top. Elevators run up to an observation deck and a revolving restaurant with a view of the city, Puget Sound, and Mount Rainier when the mountain is out. Seattle’s later skyline grew around it rather than replacing it. From the air it is a thin landmark on the edge of downtown.", fact:"The tower is 605 feet tall and was designed with earthquakes in mind."});
  set("lm:rushmore", {extract:"Mount Rushmore is a granite sculpture in the Black Hills of South Dakota, with the heads of Washington, Jefferson, Roosevelt, and Lincoln carved about 60 feet tall. Gutzon Borglum and his son Lincoln directed the work, using dynamite and jackhammers, from 1927 to 1941. From the air the pale faces sit in a dark pine hillside. The sculpture stands on land included in the 1868 Fort Laramie Treaty with the Sioux.", fact:"An unfinished Hall of Records was begun in the rock behind the heads."});
  set("lm:arch", {extract:"The Gateway Arch stands on the Mississippi riverfront in St. Louis, a stainless-steel catenary 630 feet high and 630 feet wide. Eero Saarinen designed it as a monument to westward expansion, and it opened in 1965. Small capsules climb inside the legs to a cramped observation room. From the air the arch is a bright curve on a river that still floods the grounds from time to time.", fact:"Each leg sits on bedrock about 44 feet below the surface."});
  set("lm:hoover", {extract:"Hoover Dam is a concrete arch-gravity dam in Black Canyon on the Colorado River, completed in 1936. It created Lake Mead and still feeds power into the Southwest grid. From the air the dam is a thin white curve between dark canyon walls, with the lake on one side and the river far below on the other. The Art Deco intake towers are as carefully designed as the engineering.", fact:"More than three million cubic yards of concrete were placed in the canyon after the river had been diverted."});
  set("lm:grandcyn", {extract:"The Grand Canyon is a gorge of the Colorado River in northern Arizona, up to eighteen miles wide and more than a mile deep in places. Nearly two billion years of rock are stacked in the walls. From the air the canyon is a vast, branching incision in the plateau, with rims that look almost level until the ground simply falls away. John Wesley Powell ran the river in 1869.", fact:"The Havasupai still live in a side canyon of the park."});
  set("lm:delicate", {extract:"Delicate Arch is a freestanding sandstone span in Arches National Park near Moab, Utah, about 52 feet high. It is a remnant of a fin of Entrada sandstone and has become the silhouette on Utah license plates. The walk from the viewpoint is slickrock with a wide view of the park. From the air it is a small hoop of stone on a tilted slab of desert.", fact:"Other arches in the same park have collapsed in living memory, a reminder that this one is temporary on geologic time."});
  set("lm:shiprock", {extract:"Shiprock is a volcanic neck on the Navajo Nation in northwest New Mexico, rising about 1,500 feet from the plain. It is the remnant of a volcanic pipe some 27 million years old, with radiating dikes like a stone compass. In Diné it is Tsé Bitʼaʼí, the winged rock, and climbing is not permitted. From the air it looks like a ship in one light and a factory of rock in another.", fact:"The neck is all that remains of a volcano whose surrounding cone has long since eroded away."});
  set("lm:monvalley", {extract:"Monument Valley is a Navajo Tribal Park on the Utah–Arizona line, a plateau of red sandstone buttes and mesas that became the world’s picture of the American West. John Ford filmed here so often that the mittens and a dirt road still look like a movie set. The land remains Navajo. From the air the buttes stand as isolated red islands in a huge, dusty basin under an enormous sky.", fact:"The formations have Diné names as well as the English ones that appear on postcards."});
  set("lm:yosemite", {extract:"El Capitan is a granite wall at the west end of Yosemite Valley, rising about 3,000 feet from the valley floor. Ice polished the pluton; climbers later made the Nose one of the famous big-wall routes on earth. From the air the valley is a deep, forested trough with waterfalls in spring and a granite skyline that includes Half Dome farther east. The valley was protected as a state grant in 1864, before the national park.", fact:"Half Dome’s east cables are a later, very different kind of climb from El Capitan’s walls."});
  set("lm:yellowstone", {extract:"Old Faithful is a cone geyser in Yellowstone National Park, Wyoming, sitting on a volcanic caldera that is still active. Yellowstone became the first national park in the world in 1872. The geyser’s interval is roughly an hour to two hours, not a clock. From the air the park is a high plateau of forests, a large lake, and pale hydrothermal basins that look unlike ordinary wilderness.", fact:"Wolves were restored to the park in the 1990s after decades of absence."});
  set("lm:devilstower", {extract:"Devils Tower is a phonolite butte in northeastern Wyoming, rising 867 feet from base to summit, with columnar joints that look like a pipe organ. It was the first U.S. national monument, in 1906. Several Plains nations hold it sacred and ask climbers to stay off in June. From the air it is a dark cylinder on a rolling prairie, with the Belle Fourche River looping around it.", fact:"The 1977 film Close Encounters of the Third Kind made the tower famous to a different audience."});
  set("lm:zion", {extract:"Zion is a canyon in southwestern Utah where the Virgin River has cut Navajo sandstone cliffs more than 2,000 feet high. Shuttle buses now replace summer cars in the main canyon. From the air the park is a deep red slot in the Colorado Plateau, with cottonwoods along the river and walls that go gold and rust in autumn light. Flash floods in the Narrows are the real hazard.", fact:"Angels Landing is a chain-assisted ridge walk with a drop on both sides."});
  set("lm:bryce", {extract:"Bryce Canyon is not a single canyon but a set of natural amphitheaters on the Paunsaugunt Plateau in southern Utah, packed with hoodoos of pink Claron limestone. At about 8,000 feet the air is thin and the nights are cold even in summer. From the air the amphitheaters look like eroded cities of stone along the plateau rim. Sunrise is when the color is strongest.", fact:"Ebenezer Bryce, a homesteader, is the source of the name; he is supposed to have called it a hard place to lose a cow."});
  set("lm:arches", {extract:"Arches National Park sits in high desert north of Moab, Utah, with more than 2,000 documented sandstone arches among fins, windows, and balanced rocks. Landscape Arch is one of the longest natural spans on earth. From the air the park is a maze of red fins rather than a single famous silhouette. Summers are very hot, and the Colorado River runs just south of the boundary.", fact:"Wall Arch collapsed in 2008, a reminder that the park is still changing."});
  set("lm:canyonlands", {extract:"Canyonlands occupies the confluence of the Green and Colorado rivers in southeastern Utah. Island in the Sky is a mesa between the two rivers; The Needles and The Maze are harder to reach. From the air the park is a huge, quiet country of grabens, white rim, and muddy water far below. It is larger and less visited than nearby Arches.", fact:"Uranium exploration roads from the 1950s still scar some of the benches."});
  set("lm:capitolreef", {extract:"Capitol Reef follows the Waterpocket Fold in south-central Utah, a nearly 100-mile wrinkle of warped rock named for white sandstone domes. Nineteenth-century orchards still fruit in Fruita, a tiny oasis of green. From the air the fold is a long, crumpled ridge rather than a single canyon. Flash floods own the wash roads after rain.", fact:"Fremont people left petroglyphs along the same valley that later settlers farmed."});
  set("lm:deathval", {extract:"Death Valley is a desert basin in eastern California. Badwater Basin sits 282 feet below sea level, the lowest point in North America, while Telescope Peak above it rises more than 11,000 feet. From the air the valley is a long, pale trough between dark ranges, with salt flats, dunes, and empty road. People live and work here; the name oversells the place.", fact:"A 1913 reading at Furnace Creek of 56.7 °C is often cited as a world air-temperature record."});
  set("lm:joshua", {extract:"Joshua Tree sits where the Mojave and Colorado deserts meet in southern California. Branched yuccas and granite boulders occupy the higher west; cholla and creosote take the lower east. Joshua trees are yuccas, not true trees, and they are pollinated by a moth. From the air the park is a pale, boulder-strewn highland with the basin dropping away to the south.", fact:"The park was established in 1994, comparatively late in the national park system."});
  set("lm:sequoia", {extract:"Sequoia National Park protects giant sequoia groves on the west slope of the Sierra Nevada, including the General Sherman Tree, along with high granite of the Great Western Divide. Giant sequoias grow only on this side of the range. Sherman is the largest known living single-stem tree by volume, though coast redwoods are taller. From the air the groves are dark patches of forest on a steep mountain wall above the Central Valley.", fact:"Fire is part of sequoia ecology, and managing it has become one of the park’s hardest jobs."});
  set("lm:redwood", {extract:"Redwood National and State Parks protect a remnant of coast redwood forest on the far north California shore. The trees can exceed 350 feet and need fog as well as rain. From the air the parks are a strip of dark creek and slope along the Pacific, not a single famous grove. Roosevelt elk graze the prairie bottoms.", fact:"Logging came within a generation of wiping out the tallest remaining stands."});
  set("lm:crater", {extract:"Crater Lake occupies a caldera in the Oregon Cascades formed after Mount Mazama collapsed about 7,700 years ago. Wizard Island is a cinder cone in a lake with no streams in or out at the surface — rain and snow, then seepage. The water is a blue that looks almost unreal. From the air the lake is a round, dark eye in a ring of cliffs, the deepest lake in the United States.", fact:"Snow closes the rim road for much of the year."});
  set("lm:olympic", {extract:"Olympic National Park covers glaciered peaks, a west-slope rainforest, and a wild Pacific coast of stacks and tide on Washington’s Olympic Peninsula. The Hoh rainforest can receive more than 140 inches of rain a year, while the rain shadow makes Sequim almost dry. From the air the peninsula is three landscapes at once: ice, forest, and a long, empty shore. It became a park in 1938.", fact:"The peninsula was isolated enough that some species evolved in place."});
  set("lm:nrcasc", {extract:"The North Cascades are a steep, glaciated range in northern Washington, with some of the densest ice in the lower forty-eight. Stehekin is reached by boat. The North Cascades Highway closes in winter. From the air the park is more vertical than visitable, a jumble of peaks close to the Canadian border. It is a climbers’ park more than a drive-through.", fact:"The Skagit dams in the same mountains still help light Seattle."});
  set("lm:tetons", {extract:"The Teton Range rises as a wall from Jackson Hole in Wyoming, with Grand Teton at 13,775 feet. The range is geologically young, which is why there are almost no foothills — the fault scarp is the view. From the air the peaks look drawn against a braided river and a wide, sage valley. Moose use the willows; winter belongs to the valley floor.", fact:"The park’s creation was a long political fight between ranchers, dude outfits, and monument-makers."});
  set("lm:rocky", {extract:"Rocky Mountain National Park sits on Colorado’s Front Range and straddles the Continental Divide. Trail Ridge Road tops out above 12,000 feet on alpine tundra, one of the highest paved through-roads in the United States. From the air the park is a high, lake-dotted crest close enough to Denver to be busy. Afternoon thunderstorms are a rule, not a chance.", fact:"Elk fill the meadows in autumn, and their bugling is one of the park’s seasonal sounds."});
  set("lm:mesaverde", {extract:"Mesa Verde is a park in southwestern Colorado preserving Ancestral Pueblo cliff dwellings, including Cliff Palace, built into alcoves in the thirteenth century. The people later left the mesa; drought, conflict, and migration are among the explanations. From the air the dwellings are small dark niches in a green table of juniper and piñon. Many sites are reached by ranger tour and ladders.", fact:"The name means green table, from the woodland on top of the mesa."});
  set("lm:greatsand", {extract:"Great Sand Dunes piles the tallest dunes in North America against the Sangre de Cristo Mountains in southern Colorado. Wind and a closed basin do the work. Medano Creek flows at the dune foot in spring, a beach in the mountains. From the air the dune field is a pale, shifting sea against a wall of peaks. Nights are cold; the valley is high.", fact:"Some dunes move several feet in a year."});
  set("lm:blackcyn", {extract:"The Black Canyon of the Gunnison is a narrow gorge of Precambrian rock in western Colorado. Some walls drop more than 2,000 feet with little width between them, so dark the canyon stays in shadow — hence the name. From the air it is a thin, almost black cut rather than a Grand Canyon of space. The inner canyon is expert country; the rims are an easy drive.", fact:"Painted Wall is a cliff striped with pegmatite, one of the highest cliffs in Colorado."});
  set("lm:bigbend", {extract:"Big Bend is a park on the Rio Grande in southwest Texas where the river bends around the Chisos Mountains. Desert, river, and a sky-island forest share the same huge tract of land. From the air the bend of the river is the map, with Mexico a short distance across the water. It is a dark-sky reserve, and summers belong to the mountains.", fact:"The park is larger than the state of Rhode Island."});
  set("lm:badlands", {extract:"The Badlands of southwestern South Dakota are eroded buttes and mixed-grass prairie, striped rock that erodes about an inch a year. The formations expose Oligocene fossil beds. From the air they are a pale, corrugated band next to the Pine Ridge. Sunsets turn the buttes a warm color. Bison still use the prairie.", fact:"The name comes from Lakota and French descriptions of a place that was hard to cross."});
  set("lm:acadia", {extract:"Acadia occupies Mount Desert Island and nearby coast in Maine, the first national park east of the Mississippi. Cadillac Mountain is often among the first places in the United States to see sunrise. From the air the island is granite, spruce, and a cold Atlantic, with carriage roads of crushed rock still closed to cars. Fog is a season here.", fact:"Much of the park was donated by private landowners, including carriage roads given by John D. Rockefeller Jr."});
  set("lm:everglades", {extract:"The Everglades is a slow sheet of water moving south through sawgrass and mangrove in southern Florida, a river too wide to look like one. It is the largest subtropical wilderness in the United States. From the air the park is a green, wet prairie rather than a swamp of trees everywhere. Restoration of the old sheet flow is a long civil-engineering project.", fact:"Both crocodiles and alligators occur here, a rare overlap."});
  set("lm:fortjefferson", {extract:"Fort Jefferson stands on Garden Key in the Dry Tortugas, about seventy miles west of Key West. The brick fort was never finished, and a black harbor lighthouse rises inside the walls. From the air it is a hexagon of casemates in a pale, isolated sea. There is no fresh water except what you bring.", fact:"The name Dry Tortugas refers to turtles and to the lack of fresh water."});
  set("lm:denali", {extract:"Denali is the highest peak in North America at 20,310 feet, a massif in the Alaska Range with a vertical relief that is hard to grasp from a map. The mountain was officially renamed Denali in 2015, restoring the Koyukon name. From the air it is a white pyramid that makes its own weather and can hide for weeks. Most visitors never leave the park road.", fact:"Climbing season is late spring, when the snowpack is still stable enough for the upper mountain."});
  set("lm:glacier", {extract:"Glacier National Park sits in the northern Rockies of Montana on the Canadian border. Going-to-the-Sun Road, completed in 1932, is a ledge across the divide at Logan Pass. Most of the park’s named glaciers have shrunk sharply since 1900. From the air the park is a wall of horned peaks, lakes, and a road that looks too thin for the terrain. It meets Waterton in Canada as an international peace park.", fact:"Going-to-the-Sun Road is closed by snow for more than half the year."});
  set("lm:ussarizona", {extract:"The USS Arizona Memorial spans the wreck of the battleship in Pearl Harbor, still in commission as a tomb for the crew lost on 7 December 1941. The white structure rests above the hull, and the flag flies from the ship. From the air it is a pale span on the harbor beside Ford Island. Oil still seeps from the wreck.", fact:"The Arizona was never raised. The memorial was dedicated in 1962."});
  set("lm:whitehouse", {extract:"The White House sits on Pennsylvania Avenue in Washington, D.C., the official residence and workplace of the President of the United States. John Adams was the first president to occupy it, in 1800. The British burned it in 1814; the walls still stand inside later interiors. From the air it is a pale house and lawn in a city of axial streets and large public buildings.", fact:"The West Wing is a later addition, not part of the original house."});
  set("lm:lincoln", {extract:"The Lincoln Memorial is the temple at the west end of the National Mall, finished in 1922. Daniel Chester French's seated Lincoln faces the Reflecting Pool and the Washington Monument. The wall behind him carries the inscription that this temple is a memorial for the people for whom he saved the Union. From the air it closes the long axis of the Mall.", fact:"The statue is carved from 28 blocks of Georgia marble."});
  set("lm:washmon", {extract:"The Washington Monument is an Egyptian Revival obelisk on the National Mall, 555 feet tall, completed in 1884. A change of stone color still marks the halt in construction during the Civil War. It sits on axis with the Capitol and the Lincoln Memorial. From the air it is a thin white needle in a long, green rectangle of public ground.", fact:"It was the world’s tallest structure when it was finished."});
  set("lm:alamo", {extract:"The Alamo is a former Spanish mission in San Antonio, Texas, a small church now standing at a busy intersection. The thirteen-day siege of 1836 became a founding story of the Texas Republic. From the air it is easy to miss among downtown towers; on the ground it is a stone chapel and a long barrack. Tejano as well as Anglo defenders died here.", fact:"The mission had already been secularized long before the 1836 battle."});
  set("lm:keywest", {extract:"The Southernmost Point is a painted concrete buoy in Key West, Florida, a ceremonial marker about ninety miles from Cuba. U.S. Route 1 ends here. From the air Key West is a low, crowded island at the end of a long chain of keys, closer to Havana than to Miami in miles. Hurricanes are part of the year.", fact:"The Conch Republic seceded as a joke in 1982 and kept the joke as local identity."});
  set("lm:niagara", {extract:"Niagara Falls is a set of three waterfalls on the Niagara River between New York and Ontario. Horseshoe Falls on the Canadian side carries most of the flow; American Falls and Bridal Veil sit on the U.S. side. From the air the river simply drops off a ledge into mist, with cities of motels on both banks. The falls have receded several miles upstream since the last ice age.", fact:"A 1950 treaty splits the water between power generation and the spectacle of the falls."});
  set("lm:eiffel", {extract:"The Eiffel Tower stands on the Champ de Mars in Paris, an iron lattice completed in 1889 for the Exposition Universelle. It was meant to be temporary and became the city’s letterhead. From the air it is a thin iron needle in a city of stone and river. Wind still sways the top.", fact:"Gustave Eiffel also designed the iron armature inside the Statue of Liberty."});
  set("lm:louvre", {extract:"The Louvre is a former royal palace on the Right Bank of the Seine, opened as a public museum in 1793. I. M. Pei’s glass pyramid of 1989 was controversial and then became one of the most photographed parts of the complex. From the air the palace is a huge U of roofs along the river, with the pyramid a small geometry in the courtyard. The building itself is as much the exhibit as the collection.", fact:"The Mona Lisa is smaller than many visitors expect and more heavily protected."});
  set("lm:notredame", {extract:"Notre-Dame de Paris sits on the Île de la Cité, a medieval cathedral begun in the twelfth century. A fire in 2019 destroyed the spire and much of the roof; the cathedral reopened in 2024 after a national reconstruction. From the air it is the stone ship of the island, with flying buttresses and a river on both sides. Gargoyles still throw rain off the walls.", fact:"French kings were crowned at Reims, not here; Notre-Dame was more often a church of national funerals."});
  set("lm:montstmic", {extract:"Mont-Saint-Michel is a tidal island in Normandy crowned by a medieval abbey. The mount is a granite knob in flats that can still isolate it at high tide. A causeway now reaches it. From the air it is a single pyramid of stone in a huge, pale bay. Legend dates the first oratory to the eighth century.", fact:"It was used as a prison under Napoleon."});
  set("lm:arc", {extract:"The Arc de Triomphe stands at the west end of the Champs-Élysées, a triumphal arch completed in 1836. Twelve avenues radiate from the Étoile around it. The Unknown Soldier has lain beneath it since 1921. From the air it is the hub of a star of streets in western Paris. Napoleon ordered the arch; later history filled in the rest.", fact:"Charles de Gaulle walked from the Arc in 1944 after the liberation of Paris."});
  set("lm:bigben", {extract:"Elizabeth Tower stands at the north end of the Palace of Westminster in London. Big Ben is the Great Bell, not the tower itself, though most people use the nickname for both. The clock has kept time for Parliament since 1859, with pauses for repair. From the air the tower is a thin Gothic needle on a bend of the Thames.", fact:"The tower was renamed Elizabeth Tower in 2012; Big Ben remains the name of the bell."});
  set("lm:towerbr", {extract:"Tower Bridge is a combined bascule and suspension bridge over the Thames beside the Tower of London, opened in 1894. The bascules still open for river traffic. It is not London Bridge, which is the plainer crossing upstream. From the air the two towers and the high walkways are instantly recognizable. Steam hydraulics later became oil and electricity.", fact:"The high-level walkways were once a toll crossing and later received a glass floor."});
  set("lm:stonehenge", {extract:"Stonehenge is a prehistoric circle of sarsen and bluestone on Salisbury Plain, raised in stages in the third millennium BCE. The larger stones came from the Marlborough Downs; the smaller bluestones from Wales. From the air it is a small ring in a landscape of barrows and a too-close highway. It is a complex of holes, stones, and alignments, not a single isolated circle.", fact:"No one who built it was a druid in the later, popular sense of the word."});
  set("lm:edinburgh", {extract:"Edinburgh Castle sits on Castle Rock, an extinct volcanic plug above the city. It has been royal residence, garrison, and prison. The Royal Mile runs downhill to Holyrood. From the air the rock is an obvious fortress, a dark prow of stone above a compact old town. Haar, the sea fog, still arrives from the Firth of Forth.", fact:"Mons Meg, a medieval bombard, still sits on the ramparts."});
  set("lm:giants", {extract:"The Giant’s Causeway is about 40,000 interlocking basalt columns on the north coast of County Antrim, formed as lava cooled and shrank. The same flow appears at Fingal’s Cave in Scotland. From the air the columns are a dark, geometric shore against a violent sea. Legend says Finn MacCool built a causeway to Scotland; geology says cooling basalt.", fact:"The site is in Northern Ireland, on a coast that takes the full force of the Atlantic."});
  set("lm:colosseum", {extract:"The Colosseum is an elliptical amphitheatre in central Rome, completed under Titus in 80 CE. It could hold tens of thousands, and the hypogeum under the floor was a machine of lifts and animals. Earthquakes took the south side; later centuries used it as a quarry and a fortress. From the air it is still huge, an oval of travertine in the middle of the city.", fact:"In its early years the arena could be flooded for mock sea battles."});
  set("lm:pisa", {extract:"The Leaning Tower is the campanile of Pisa Cathedral, a white marble bell tower on the Field of Miracles. The tilt began before the tower was finished, on soft ground. Stabilization in the 1990s reduced the lean to a safer angle. From the air it is a small, tilted cylinder on a green lawn beside the cathedral. The bells still work.", fact:"The story of Galileo dropping weights from the tower is a good one with a shaky source."});
  set("lm:venice", {extract:"St Mark’s Basilica faces Piazza San Marco in Venice, a Byzantine-Italian church faced with mosaics and eastern marbles. It began as the doge’s chapel. The campanile fell in 1902 and was rebuilt as it was. From the air the basilica is a low, ornate cluster at the head of the Grand Canal, in a city without ordinary streets. Acqua alta still floods the piazza.", fact:"The four bronze horses on the façade are copies; the originals are kept inside."});
  set("lm:duomo", {extract:"Milan Cathedral is a vast Gothic church begun in 1386 and finished, in a sense, in the twentieth century. The roof is a forest of spires that can be walked. From the air it is a white marble mountain in the middle of a dense city. The gold Madonnina at the top is a local landmark. Fog and trams still meet at the door.", fact:"The last ceremonial gate was set as late as 1965, nearly six centuries after the first stone."});
  set("lm:vesuvius", {extract:"Mount Vesuvius is a stratovolcano on the Bay of Naples, the only active volcano on the European mainland. Pompeii and Herculaneum were buried in 79 CE. It last erupted in 1944. From the air it is a cone over a caldera, with a huge city around its feet and a wine-growing slope. The crater hike is ash and a view of the bay.", fact:"Pliny the Younger wrote the famous eyewitness letter describing the 79 CE eruption."});
  set("lm:etna", {extract:"Mount Etna is an active stratovolcano on the east coast of Sicily, the highest volcano in Europe south of the Caucasus. Ski lifts and lava fields share the mountain. Catania has been destroyed more than once. From the air it is a huge, smoking massif with oranges and towns below the snow. Ash can close the airport on short notice.", fact:"It has been in near-continuous activity for thousands of years."});
  set("lm:sagrada", {extract:"The Sagrada Família is a basilica in Barcelona designed by Antoni Gaudí. Work began in 1882 and continues, with eighteen planned spires. The Nativity and Passion façades belong to different centuries of style. From the air it is a cluster of stone towers in the city’s grid, still unfinished and already the skyline. The interior is a forest of branched columns and colored light.", fact:"It is not Barcelona’s cathedral — the diocese already had one."});
  set("lm:alhambra", {extract:"The Alhambra is a palace and fortress above Granada, developed by the Nasrid emirs in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries. Stucco, water, and gardens occupy a ridge with a view of the Albaicín. Ferdinand and Isabella took it in 1492. From the air it is a red citadel on a green hill above a compact city. Fountains were the air conditioning of a dry kingdom.", fact:"Washington Irving’s Tales of the Alhambra helped make the palace famous in English."});
  set("lm:acropolis", {extract:"The Acropolis is the high citadel of ancient Athens. The Parthenon was begun in 447 BCE, Pentelic marble on a limestone plateau that was already a Mycenaean fort. From the air it is a flat rock of ruins above a dense modern city. Restoration has been a century of scaffolding. Pollution and visitors still wear the stone.", fact:"One of the Erechtheion’s caryatids on the porch is a copy; the original is in the museum."});
  set("lm:santorini", {extract:"Santorini is a flooded volcanic caldera in the southern Aegean. A Bronze Age eruption helped shape the present bay. White houses sit on the lip of dark cliffs that drop to deep water. From the air the island is a broken ring around a sea-filled crater. Akrotiri, the buried town, is the serious archaeology beneath the postcard.", fact:"The inner cliffs of Thera drop almost sheer to a caldera hundreds of meters deep."});
  set("lm:neusch", {extract:"Neuschwanstein is a nineteenth-century palace in the Bavarian Alps, commissioned by Ludwig II on a crag above the Pöllat Gorge. Ludwig never saw it finished. From the air it is a pale castle on a wooded knob, with Hohenschwangau, his father’s house, in the valley below. Fog can make it look older than it is. Interior rooms are few and ornate.", fact:"The look of the palace later helped shape the popular idea of a fairy-tale castle."});
  set("lm:brandenburg", {extract:"The Brandenburg Gate is a neoclassical arch on Pariser Platz in Berlin, completed in 1793. The quadriga was taken to Paris by Napoleon and later returned. The gate stood in the death strip of a divided city and reopened as a crossing in 1989. From the air it is a modest sandstone arch at the hinge of the city’s old west.", fact:"The Reichstag is only a short walk from the gate."});
  set("lm:cologne", {extract:"Cologne Cathedral is a High Gothic church on the Rhine. Medieval cranes stood on the unfinished nave for centuries; the cathedral was completed in 1880 to medieval plans. Allied bombing flattened much of the city and left the Dom standing. From the air it is a dark twin-spired landmark on the river, still a navigation mark. The square is a station exit.", fact:"It houses the Shrine of the Three Kings, a medieval reliquary at the heart of the church."});
  set("lm:matterhorn", {extract:"The Matterhorn is a 4,478-meter pyramidal peak on the Swiss–Italian border above Zermatt. Four of the first ascent party died on the way down in 1865. From the air the shape is almost too simple, a sharp pyramid above a car-free town that helped invent alpine tourism. The Hörnli ridge is the classic route. Glaciers on its flanks have receded.", fact:"The Gornergrat railway is the easy way to a view that used to require a climb."});
  set("lm:atomium", {extract:"The Atomium in Brussels is an iron crystal magnified about 165 billion times, built for Expo 58 and kept afterward. Each sphere is 18 meters across. Escalators in tubes connect the atoms, and a restaurant occupies the top. From the air it is a silver molecule on the Heysel plateau, not in the old city. It was meant to be temporary.", fact:"Night lighting turns the structure into a kind of science-fiction lamp over the park."});
  set("lm:keukenhof", {extract:"Keukenhof is a seasonal display garden at Lisse in South Holland, open only in spring. It opened to the public in 1949 as a showcase of the bulb district. Millions of bulbs are planted each year in a landscape of controlled color. From the air, in season, it is a rectangle of stripes and blocks in the polder. The name means kitchen garden.", fact:"The display is horticulture as spectacle, not a wild flower meadow."});
  set("lm:sagrada2", {extract:"Park Güell sits on Carmel Hill in Barcelona, a garden designed by Antoni Gaudí for Eusebi Güell. Mosaic benches, a salamander fountain, and a terrace look over the city. The original housing estate was never fully built; the park became municipal. From the air it is a green hill of curves above the grid. The columns of the hypostyle hall are a stone forest.", fact:"UNESCO later listed the park as part of Gaudí’s civic work."});
  set("lm:sintra", {extract:"Pena Palace sits above Sintra, Portugal, a Romanticist collage of color rebuilt in the 1840s for Ferdinand II on the site of a monastery. Fog and eucalyptus wrap the hill. From the air it is a bright toy castle in a green Serra, with Lisbon a train ride away. The walk to the Moorish castle is another ticket.", fact:"The surrounding cultural landscape of quintas and forest is itself a UNESCO site."});
  set("lm:belem", {extract:"Belém Tower is a sixteenth-century fortified tower on the Tagus at Lisbon, built to guard the river mouth in the age of oceanic voyages. It looks like a stone ship. From the air it is a small fortress in a wide river that is almost the sea, with container ships now in the same water the caravels used. The nearby Jerónimos Monastery is the other half of the shore.", fact:"UNESCO lists the tower together with the monastery where Vasco da Gama is buried."});
  set("lm:giza", {extract:"The Great Pyramid of Giza is the tomb of Khufu, built in the twenty-sixth century BCE on a plateau that is now the edge of Cairo. About 2.3 million blocks went into it. It is the last standing monument of the classical Seven Wonders. From the air the three pyramids and the Sphinx sit on desert immediately west of a vast modern city. The interior is a steep corridor to a granite room.", fact:"Khufu’s pyramid held the record as the world’s tallest structure for millennia."});
  set("lm:sphinx", {extract:"The Great Sphinx is a recumbent limestone figure on the Giza Plateau, generally dated to Khafre, with a lion’s body and a worn human face. The Dream Stele of Thutmose IV sits between the paws. From the air it is smaller than the pyramids and more horizontal, a low guardian in the sand. Restoration has been a long argument in stone and concrete.", fact:"The missing nose was already gone in early modern drawings; blaming Napoleon’s gunners is a myth."});
  set("lm:luxor", {extract:"Karnak is a temple complex at Thebes, modern Luxor, grown over many reigns rather than one. The hypostyle hall is a forest of stone columns. It is not Luxor Temple, which stands farther down the avenue of sphinxes. From the air Karnak is a huge rectangle of ruins on the east bank of the Nile, with the Valley of the Kings on the west. Heat is a character here.", fact:"Hot-air balloons at dawn have become a modern ritual over the Theban west bank."});
  set("lm:tablemtn", {extract:"Table Mountain is a flat-topped sandstone plateau overlooking Cape Town, a mesa that defined a sea route long before it became a park. Cloud over the top is called the tablecloth. From the air the mountain is a wall behind the city, harbor, and peninsula, with two oceans when the air is clean. Dassies own the rocks. Fires sweep the fynbos.", fact:"The cableway opened in 1929 and still runs to the plateau."});
  set("lm:vfalls", {extract:"Victoria Falls is a curtain of the Zambezi on the Zambia–Zimbabwe border, dropping into a basalt gorge. The local name Mosi-oa-Tunya means The Smoke That Thunders. In the dry season the curtain can be thin; in the wet it is blinding. From the air the river simply disappears into a slot of spray. Mist feeds a rainforest in the gorge.", fact:"A 1905 steel-arch bridge still spans the gorge below the falls."});
  set("lm:kili", {extract:"Kilimanjaro is a dormant volcanic massif in Tanzania and the highest mountain in Africa at 5,895 meters. Most climbers use the Machame or Marangu routes; altitude, not technical ice, is the main problem. From the air it is a free-standing white dome above savanna, visible from a plane on a clear morning. The ice cap has been shrinking for decades.", fact:"Five ecological zones run from banana farms at the base to the crater at the top."});
  set("lm:sahara", {extract:"The pyramids of Meroë are a royal cemetery of the Kingdom of Kush on a desert bend of the Nile in Sudan, used from about 300 BCE to 350 CE. They are steeper than the pyramids at Giza and far less visited. From the air they are small, sharp points of brick and stone in open desert. Meroitic script is still not fully read.", fact:"Hundreds of these tombs survive, a former empire’s graveyard rather than a single famous triangle."});
  set("lm:taj", {extract:"The Taj Mahal is a white marble mausoleum at Agra on the Yamuna, built in the mid-seventeenth century by Shah Jahan for Mumtaz Mahal. Four minarets and a charbagh garden complete the composition. From the air it is a pale dome on a river terrace, with red sandstone buildings flanking it. Pollution yellows the stone; mud-pack cleaning is one of the treatments. Sunrise is the usual hour to see it.", fact:"The true graves are below; the cenotaphs in the main chamber are the ones visitors see."});
  set("lm:redfort", {extract:"The Red Fort is a sandstone fortress-palace in Delhi, begun by Shah Jahan in 1639 when the Mughal capital moved from Agra. Many halls inside were marble. From the air the red walls are a large rectangle pressed against Old Delhi. Lahori Gate is the usual postcard. Evening light makes the sandstone look warm.", fact:"The Indian prime minister addresses the nation from its ramparts on Independence Day."});
  set("lm:gwall", {extract:"Mutianyu is a restored Ming section of the Great Wall northeast of Beijing, with close-set watchtowers on a forested ridge. The Ming rebuilt much of the wall that visitors see; earlier walls were often tamped earth. From the air it is a thin brick line following the mountains, not a single endless barrier. Autumn color on the hills is a fine time to go.", fact:"It is not visible from the moon with the unaided eye — that is a later myth."});
  set("lm:forbid", {extract:"The Forbidden City is the imperial palace of the Ming and Qing in Beijing, a walled city of halls and courtyards used from 1420 until 1924. Nearly a thousand buildings sit on a north–south axis from Tiananmen to the rear gardens. From the air it is a rectangle of yellow roofs inside a moat, a city inside the city. Winter is the quieter season.", fact:"The last emperor left; the complex is now a museum."});
  set("lm:fuji", {extract:"Mount Fuji is an active stratovolcano and Japan’s highest peak at 3,776 meters. The present cone is only a few thousand years old. From the air it is a nearly perfect volcanic triangle, with five lakes on the north and, on rare clear days, a view from Tokyo. The official climbing season on the main routes is summer. The last eruption was 1707.", fact:"Hokusai’s prints made the mountain a national image long before mass tourism."});
  set("lm:tokyo", {extract:"Tokyo Tower is an orange-and-white lattice communications tower in Minato, completed in 1958 and 333 meters tall. It was the tallest structure in Japan when it opened. Tokyo Skytree is taller now; the older tower still photographs well against cherry trees. From the air it is a thin, painted needle in a city that rebuilt after firebombing.", fact:"The paint scheme follows aviation safety colors rather than a decorative choice alone."});
  set("lm:itsuku", {extract:"The grand torii of Itsukushima Shrine stands in the tidal water off Miyajima, Hiroshima Prefecture. The shrine halls are rebuilt periodically in the Shinto tradition and sit on piers over a tidal flat. At low tide you can walk to the gate; at high tide it floats in the view. From the air the vermilion gate is a small mark against a green holy hill. Deer wander the island.", fact:"UNESCO lists the shrine; Hiroshima is a short train and ferry away."});
  set("lm:petronas", {extract:"The Petronas Towers are twin skyscrapers in Kuala Lumpur completed in 1998, joined by a skybridge at floors 41 and 42. They were once the world’s tallest buildings and remain the tallest twin towers. From the air they are a paired stainless geometry that still organizes the city’s skyline. Night lighting is a civic event. They sit on a former race course.", fact:"Architect César Pelli’s brief was to make the towers look like Malaysia."});
  set("lm:angkor", {extract:"Angkor Wat is a twelfth-century temple city near Siem Reap, built by Suryavarman II as a Hindu temple to Vishnu. The moat is a rectangle of sky. Bas-reliefs of the Mahabharata run for hundreds of meters. From the air it is a huge, moated square of stone in forest, the largest religious monument by some measures of area. Sunrise at the reflecting pools is the usual photograph.", fact:"The Khmer empire’s hydraulic city around the temple is still being mapped."});
  set("lm:marina", {extract:"Marina Bay Sands is a hotel and observation deck on Marina Bay in Singapore, opened in 2010. Three towers support a long rooftop platform, with an infinity pool that became the building’s image. From the air it looks like a ship parked on the downtown waterfront, beside gardens and a bay of reclaimed water. Heat and air-conditioning define the climate at ground level.", fact:"The rooftop platform is longer than the Eiffel Tower is tall."});
  set("lm:burj", {extract:"The Burj Khalifa in Dubai stands 828 meters, a buttressed spire opened in 2010. Most of that height is spectacle as much as occupancy. From the air it is a thin needle far above a city of highways, desert, and coastline. At the top the wind and the curve of the earth are the show. Night is when the tower makes the most sense from the ground.", fact:"Architect Adrian Smith drew the plan from a desert flower and from Islamic geometry."});
  set("lm:palm", {extract:"Palm Jumeirah is an artificial archipelago in the Persian Gulf off Dubai, built in the shape of a palm tree and crescent. The fronds are dredged sand; the breakwater is a crescent. From the air it is a logo of land in blue water. From the road it is gates, villas, and landscaping. It is a city that invented more shoreline.", fact:"Work on the palm began in 2001."});
  set("lm:petra", {extract:"Petra is the rock-cut capital of the Nabataeans in present-day Jordan. Al-Khazneh, the Treasury, is a façade at the end of the Siq, a sandstone canyon that is the real approach. Tombs, a theatre, and a monastery up a long stair complete the site. From the air the city is hidden in a maze of wadis until you are almost on it. The Nabataeans piped water through a desert trading town.", fact:"Johann Ludwig Burckhardt brought the site to wide European attention in 1812."});
  set("lm:istanbul", {extract:"Hagia Sophia sits on the old acropolis of Byzantium, a sixth-century church built under Justinian I, later a mosque, a museum, and a mosque again. The dome defined Byzantine space; the minarets are later. From the air it is a huge, lead-roofed mass facing the Golden Horn. Interior mosaics survive in patches. Crowds are constant.", fact:"The church was completed in 537, an extraordinary speed for a building of that size."});
  set("lm:capp", {extract:"Cappadocia is a volcanic region of central Turkey known for fairy chimneys, underground cities, and rock-cut churches around Göreme. People have lived in the carved tuff for centuries. From the air the valleys look almost unreal, a landscape of cones and pigeon houses. Balloons at dawn are the modern harvest. Winter can put snow on the dust.", fact:"The frescoes in Göreme are a Byzantine gallery cut into the rock itself."});
  set("lm:opera", {extract:"The Sydney Opera House sits on Bennelong Point, designed by Jørn Utzon and opened in 1973. The shells are covered in more than a million tiles and look like sails or orange segments depending on the light. Utzon resigned before it was finished. From the air it is a white cluster beside the Harbour Bridge, the national image of the city. Performances inside are the point that photographs forget.", fact:"The building ran late and over budget, and became the national emblem anyway."});
  set("lm:harbour", {extract:"The Sydney Harbour Bridge is a steel through-arch opened in 1932, linking the north and south shores of Port Jackson. It still carries rail, road, and pedestrians. From the air it is the older half of the famous pair with the Opera House. Painting it is a continuous job. Ferries still pass underneath.", fact:"The arch was built from both shores and joined in the middle."});
  set("lm:uluru", {extract:"Uluru is a large sandstone inselberg in Australia’s Northern Territory, sacred to the Anangu. It changes color with the sun, which is as accurate as it sounds. The climb closed in 2019 at the request of traditional owners. From the air it is a single red whale of rock in a huge, flat center. Waterholes and rock art line the base walk.", fact:"The surrounding land is a national park jointly managed with the Anangu."});
  set("lm:gbrier", {extract:"Cairns is the main city for the northern Great Barrier Reef, a system of thousands of reefs and islands off Queensland. From the air the reef is a different color of sea, not a single wall of coral. Boats, snorkels, and a long argument about love and damage leave from here. Cyclones break and rebuild. Bleaching events are the crisis of the present.", fact:"It is the world’s largest coral system, visible from orbit as a change in the color of the water."});
  set("lm:hobbiton", {extract:"Hobbiton is a film set left standing as a village of round doors on a sheep farm near Matamata in the Waikato of New Zealand. The first set was built in 1999 and later rebuilt as a permanent village. From the air it is a green hill of holes in working pasture. Tours are timed. Rain makes it look more like the Shire, which is frequent.", fact:"The party tree on the hill is a real tree, not a prop."});
  set("lm:milford", {extract:"Milford Sound is a drowned glacial valley in Fiordland on New Zealand’s South Island, with Mitre Peak rising more than a mile from the water. Piopiotahi is the Māori name. Rainfall is among the highest on earth, which is why the waterfalls work. From the air the sound is a dark, steep slot of water between wet beech walls. Sandflies are the tax. The road in is a tunnel and a pass.", fact:"Geologically it is a fiord, a glacial valley drowned by the sea."});
  set("lm:redeemer", {extract:"Christ the Redeemer stands on Corcovado above Rio de Janeiro, a reinforced-concrete statue dedicated in 1931, arms open to a city of bay and hills. Cloud often eats the head. From the air the figure is a small white cross on a granite peak above beaches and forest. The train up the mountain is the old way. Art Deco folds in the robe.", fact:"Lightning has struck the statue many times."});
  set("lm:sugarloaf", {extract:"Sugarloaf Mountain is a granite peak at the mouth of Guanabara Bay in Rio, 396 meters high and reached by cable car. The name compares it to a loaf of sugar in a mold. From the air it is a rounded monolith at the harbor mouth, smaller than Corcovado and better as a ship-from-the-sea. Two stages of gondola make the visit. Climbers treat the faces as slabs.", fact:"The first cable car opened in 1912."});
  set("lm:iguazu", {extract:"Iguazu Falls is a system of cataracts on the Iguazu River along the Argentina–Brazil border. Garganta del Diablo is the U-shaped main drop. National parks occupy both sides. From the air the river spreads into a wide horseshoe of falling water and spray. Coatimundis are bold around picnic tables. Flood and drought change the width.", fact:"The Argentine walkways get closer to the water; the Brazilian side is known for the panorama."});
  set("lm:machu", {extract:"Machu Picchu is a fifteenth-century Inca estate on a ridge above the Urubamba in Peru, abandoned after the Spanish conquest. Terraces, temples, and a saddle of cloud forest make the site. From the air it is a stone village on a green knife of mountain, with higher peaks beyond. Permits cap the day. Stones fit without mortar. Morning mist is the postcard.", fact:"Hiram Bingham brought it to wide outside attention in 1911, though local farmers already knew the ridge."});
  set("lm:chichen", {extract:"Chichen Itza is a large Maya city in Yucatán, famous for El Castillo, a temple to Kukulcán. Cenotes, a ball court, and iguanas share the site. Some of the restoration is imaginative. From the air the pyramid is a geometric mound in dry forest, not far from the tour corridor to the coast. Heat is drier here than on the Caribbean shore.", fact:"At the equinox, a shadow on the stair is said to look like a serpent."});
  set("lm:teoti", {extract:"Teotihuacan is a pre-Aztec city northeast of Mexico City, known for the Pyramids of the Sun and Moon and the Avenue of the Dead. The Aztecs found it already ruinous and called it the place of the gods. From the air it is a huge, ordered grid of pyramids and empty streets in a high valley. Climbing the Sun pyramid is now restricted. The scale is imperial.", fact:"The city may have held as many as 100,000 people at its height."});
  set("lm:cancunm", {extract:"El Castillo at Tulum is a late Maya cliff temple on the Caribbean coast of Quintana Roo, looking over the sea from a small walled town. It is not Chichen Itza; it is a beach ruin. From the air the castle is a tiny stone block on a limestone bluff above turquoise water. Iguanas own the walls. Morning is the quieter hour. The site is fragile and crowded.", fact:"Tulum was a port facing the reef, not a great inland capital."});
  set("lm:cn", {extract:"The CN Tower is a concrete communications and observation tower in Toronto, completed in 1976 and 553 meters tall. It held the title of world’s tallest free-standing structure for three decades. From the air it is a thin needle on the lake shore, with the downtown skyline grown around it. Lake Ontario is the south view. The revolving restaurant is a 1970s future that still works.", fact:"A glass floor in the lower observation deck is part of the visit."});
  set("lm:banff", {extract:"Moraine Lake sits in the Valley of the Ten Peaks in Banff National Park, Alberta. Glacial rock flour colors the water. Banff was Canada’s first national park, established in 1885. From the air the lake is a turquoise comma under a wall of peaks, with a rockpile at one end. Parking is now managed because the view is so famous. Both black and grizzly bears live here.", fact:"The town of Banff began as a railway spa in a valley of hotels and elk."});
  set("lm:peyto", {extract:"Peyto Lake is a glacier-fed lake in Banff National Park, seen from Bow Summit on the Icefields Parkway. Rock flour colors the water a blue that looks edited. From the air it is a turquoise finger in a hanging valley, with no town nearby. Crowds gather at midday; first light is emptier. The glacier that feeds it is receding, which will change the color eventually.", fact:"The viewpoint is a short walk from the parkway, which is why so many people know the picture."});
  set("lm:moai", {extract:"The moai of Rapa Nui are monolithic human figures carved from tuff, most of them from the quarry at Rano Raraku. About 900 were carved. Most ahu face inland, so the living watched the ancestors watch them. From the air the unfinished giants still lie in the quarry slope, and the restored rows stand on the coast of a small, remote island. The hats are red scoria.", fact:"Carving stopped for reasons still argued: ecology, conflict, and introduced rats among them."});
  set("lm:torres", {extract:"Torres del Paine is a park in Chilean Patagonia named for three granite towers of the Paine Massif, with lakes and glaciers around them. Wind is the climate. From the air the horns of granite rise above glacial water, an emblem of a 1959 park. Guanacos use the grasslands. Puerto Natales is the usual gate. Dawn can turn the towers orange for a few minutes.", fact:"The W trek is the walking route that made the park an industry."});
  set("lm:easter2", {extract:"Ahu Tongariki is the largest ahu on Rapa Nui, with fifteen restored moai facing inland from the coast near the quarry of Rano Raraku. A tsunami had knocked the row down; it was re-erected in the 1990s. From the air the fifteen figures are a single line of stone with empty ocean at their backs. Sunrise is the photograph. Restoration used cranes, which the original engineers did not.", fact:"It has become the island’s most famous postcard, more than any single standing moai."});
  set("lm:moscow", {extract:"Saint Basil’s Cathedral stands on Red Square in Moscow, commissioned by Ivan IV and completed in 1561, a cluster of chapels with onion domes. The interior is smaller than the exterior promises. From the air it is a colored fire of domes against the Kremlin wall. Stalin almost demolished it. Winter snow turns it into something like a toy, which does not make it less serious.", fact:"It is not the patriarchal cathedral; that role moved elsewhere."});
  set("lm:hermitage", {extract:"The Winter Palace on Palace Square in Saint Petersburg is the former residence of Russian emperors and now part of the Hermitage Museum. Catherine II began the collection that fills the palace and its neighbors. From the air it is a long green-and-white baroque wall on the Neva. The Jordan staircase is a coup of marble. You cannot see it in a day.", fact:"During the siege of Leningrad the collection was evacuated; the building itself suffered."});
  set("lm:kremlin", {extract:"The Moscow Kremlin is a fortified complex on the Moskva River, a seat of Russian power for centuries, with cathedrals and palaces inside brick walls that are mostly fifteenth century. From the air it is a triangle of walls, gold domes, and gardens on a river bend, with Red Square along one side. It is a working citadel, not only a museum. Tours are routed.", fact:"Red stars on the towers replaced earlier double-headed eagles."});
  set("lm:nordkapp", {extract:"North Cape is a high cliff on Magerøya in northern Norway, long presented as a northern extremity of Europe, though a nearby knoll is slightly farther north. A globe monument and a visitor center stand on the plateau. From the air the cliff is a dark, flat-topped wall above the Arctic Ocean. The midnight sun appears in summer; polar night in winter. A tunnel reaches the island.", fact:"German forces fortified the cape in the Second World War."});
  set("lm:trolltunga", {extract:"Trolltunga is a horizontal tongue of rock about 700 meters above Ringedalsvatnet near Tyssedal in Vestland, Norway. The name means troll tongue. The hike is a long day. From the air the ledge is a tiny diving board of granite over a lake in a glaciated valley. Weather closes the trail without much warning. Odda is the old industrial gateway.", fact:"The ledge is granite left behind by ice, not a constructed platform."});
  set("lm:geiranger", {extract:"Geirangerfjord is a branch of Storfjorden in western Norway, a steep drowned valley of farms on ledges and waterfalls named the Seven Sisters. Cruise ships are the economy and a pressure on the place. From the air the fjord is a green, narrow slot of deep water between walls that work from a ship’s deck. Eagle Road switchbacks above the village. Rain is frequent.", fact:"It is part of a UNESCO listing with Nærøyfjord."});
  set("lm:hallgrim", {extract:"Hallgrímskirkja is a Lutheran church in Reykjavík designed by Guðjón Samúelsson and completed in 1986. The façade is meant to recall basalt columns. The tower dominates a low city of colorful roofs and a grey sea. From the air it is a concrete spire in the middle of a small capital. Wind on the observation deck is not a figure of speech.", fact:"A statue of Leif Erikson stands outside, a gift from the United States."});
  set("lm:gulfoss", {extract:"Gullfoss is a two-stage waterfall of the Hvítá in southwest Iceland, dropping into a canyon on the Golden Circle. Mist and a trail that ices in winter are part of the visit. From the air the river simply elbows into a hidden gorge. Rainbows form in the spray. The name means golden falls. The canyon is the surprise after the lip.", fact:"Sigríður Tómasdóttir is credited in local story with helping save the falls from a hydroelectric scheme."});
  set("lm:blue", {extract:"The Blue Lagoon is a geothermal spa in a lava field near Grindavík, Iceland, opened to the public in 1992. The water is runoff from the Svartsengi geothermal plant, milky with silica. It is not a wild natural pool. From the air it is a pale, irregular pond in dark lava on the Reykjanes peninsula. Eruptions in the 2020s put visits on pause more than once.", fact:"White silica mud masks are part of the bath, not a separate attraction."});
  set("lm:borobudur", {extract:"Borobudur is a ninth-century Mahayana Buddhist monument in Central Java, terraces and stupas covering a hill with narrative reliefs. It was buried under ash and jungle until the nineteenth century. From the air it is a stone pyramid of rings in a green plain near Yogyakarta. Sunrise from the hill is a ticketed ritual. Islam is the majority religion now; the monument is a national one.", fact:"It dates to about 825, in the Sailendra period."});
  set("lm:prambanan", {extract:"Prambanan is a ninth-century Hindu temple compound in Central Java dedicated chiefly to Shiva, with a tall central tower. A major reconstruction followed a 2006 earthquake. From the air the spires are a cluster of sharp prangs in a dry park near Yogyakarta, not far from Borobudur and a different theology. Many smaller shrines are still piles of stone. Ramayana ballet is performed on some nights.", fact:"The joints of modern reconstruction are still visible if you look for them."});
  set("lm:komodo", {extract:"Komodo is an Indonesian island in East Nusa Tenggara, habitat of the Komodo dragon, in a national park with Rinca and Padar. The park was founded in 1980. From the air the islands are dry hills, pink beaches, and a coral sea. Labuan Bajo on Flores is the usual gate. Dragons are the largest lizards on earth. Boats, heat, and a ranger are the usual visit.", fact:"Feeding sites that once made the dragons gather on cue are now restricted."});
  set("lm:bali", {extract:"Tanah Lot is a sea temple on a rock off the southwest coast of Bali, one of seven sea temples around the island. The name means land in the sea. Priests still work the ceremonies; sunset draws a crowd. From the air it is a small shrine on a volcanic rock in the tide, west of the tourist south. Waves are the other congregation.", fact:"Offerings of flowers remain part of the temple’s daily life."});
  set("lm:ha-long", {extract:"Hạ Long Bay is a stretch of the Gulf of Tonkin in Quảng Ninh, Vietnam, filled with limestone islands and sea caves. The name is often explained as descending dragon. From the air the bay is thousands of dark karst towers in pale water, with junks among them. Overnight boats are the usual visit. Haze is frequent and can be the best light. Kayaks reach quieter lagoons.", fact:"Fishing villages on rafts have been moved as tourism grew."});
  set("lm:angkor2", {extract:"Bayon is the state temple of Jayavarman VII at Angkor Thom, known for stone faces on its towers and reliefs of Khmer life and war. The faces are often identified with the king or with Lokesvara. From the air it is a crowded stone mountain at the center of a walled city, stranger than Angkor Wat. Ta Prohm, the tree-root temple, is a cousin nearby. Heat gathers in the enclosures.", fact:"It is a Buddhist temple-mountain, not a later copy of Angkor Wat."});
  set("lm:everest", {extract:"Mount Everest is the highest mountain on earth at 8,849 meters, on the Nepal–Tibet border. The first confirmed ascent was Hillary and Tenzing in 1953. From the air it is a pyramid in a wall of greater Himalaya, with the Khumbu a valley of lodges, porters, and ice. The icefall on the south side is the technical horror of the usual route. Climate change opens and closes lines.", fact:"Namche Bazaar is the economic center of the approach from Nepal."});
  set("lm:potala", {extract:"The Potala Palace in Lhasa was the winter residence of the Dalai Lamas from the seventeenth century until 1959, a white and red palace above the city. A thousand rooms sit at about 3,700 meters. From the air it is a ziggurat of walls on a ridge, with a much larger modern city around it. Pilgrims still circuit. Stairs hurt at this altitude.", fact:"The present palace rose in the seventeenth century on a site of older fortresses."});
  set("lm:jeju", {extract:"Seongsan Ilchulbong is a tuff cone on the east shore of Jeju Island, South Korea, formed by a hydrovolcanic eruption and climbed for sunrise. The crater is a grassy bowl. From the air it is a green cup stuck on the edge of the island, with the sea on three sides. Tour buses come at dawn. Haenyeo women still dive the nearby coast.", fact:"Sunrise from the rim is a Korean New Year tradition."});
  set("lm:nseoul", {extract:"N Seoul Tower stands on Namsan in central Seoul, a communications and observation tower opened in 1980. Locks of love crowd the railings. From the air it is a needle on a hill in the middle of a basin of apartment ridges. Night is the better view. The old city walls pass nearby. Smog is often part of the air.", fact:"A cable car runs up Namsan from the dense city below."});
  set("lm:taipei101", {extract:"Taipei 101 is a skyscraper in Taipei completed in 2004, 508 meters to the tip, a stacked pagoda of glass. A giant tuned mass damper hangs in the upper floors, a gold sphere that sways to keep the tower still in typhoons and quakes. From the air it is a bamboo stalk of a building in a basin of city. Night markets sit at its foot.", fact:"It was the world’s tallest building from 2004 to 2010."});
  set("lm:hkpeak", {extract:"Victoria Peak is the famous high point on Hong Kong Island, 552 meters, with a deck looking over a working harbor. The Peak Tram opened in 1888. Fog can erase the city. From the air the peak is a green ridge above one of the densest shorelines on earth. Night is why many people go: a harbor of light. Reclamation has narrowed the water since the old photographs.", fact:"It is not the highest point on Hong Kong Island, but it is the one with the view that made the name."});
  set("lm:moai2", {extract:"Ahu Akivi is an ahu on Easter Island with seven moai that face the sea, unlike most platforms on the island. They stand inland rather than on a cliff. Oral tradition says they look toward the ancestral homeland. From the air they are a small, even row in pasture and scoria, with fewer visitors than Tongariki. Sunset lights their faces.", fact:"A 1960 restoration put these seven figures back on their ahu."});
  set("lm:charles", {founded:"1357", extract:"Charles Bridge crosses the Vltava in Prague, a stone Gothic bridge begun in 1357 under Charles IV. Towers stand at both ends, and a line of baroque statues was added later along the parapet. From the air it is a long, occupied span between the Old Town and the castle hill, still a walking street rather than a road. Floods have taken statues and piers more than once.", fact:"Until 1841 it was the only crossing of the Vltava in Prague."});
  set("lm:chrysler", {founded:"1930", extract:"The Chrysler Building is an Art Deco skyscraper in Midtown Manhattan, completed in 1930 with a stainless-steel crown of sunbursts and a needle. It held the title of world’s tallest building for a few months before the Empire State Building opened. From the air the terraced spire is easy to pick out among later glass. The gargoyles are eagles, not monsters.", fact:"The crown was assembled in secret so a rival tower would not know the final height."});
  set("lm:grand", {founded:"1913", extract:"Grand Central Terminal is a Beaux-Arts station in Midtown Manhattan, opened in 1913 as the New York Central’s terminus. The main concourse is a civic room under a starry ceiling, with suburban and long-distance tracks stacked below the street. From the air it is a low, busy block of roofs and light wells rather than a tower. Trains still run; the building is not a museum of itself.", fact:"The ceiling of the concourse is painted with a reversed zodiac, a famous error that was kept."});
  set("lm:inari", {founded:"711", extract:"Fushimi Inari Taisha sits on a wooded hill in southern Kyoto, a shrine to Inari with thousands of vermilion torii forming tunnels up the mountain. The inner shrine dates to the eighth century; the gates are later gifts from merchants and worshippers. From the air the torii are a thin orange line through trees rather than a single famous building. Foxes, Inari’s messengers, appear as stone guardians along the path.", fact:"There is no single count of the gates; new ones are still dedicated."});
  set("lm:matsumae", {founded:"1854", extract:"Matsumae Castle, also called Fukuyama Castle, stands at the southern tip of Hokkaido, the only traditional Japanese castle on the island. The present keep is a 1960 reconstruction of a late-Edo tower that burned after the war. From the air it is a white keep on a green park above the Tsugaru Strait, with cherry trees that draw a spring crowd. The Matsumae clan held this coast as a gate to Ezo.", fact:"It is the northernmost castle built in the Japanese style."});
  set("lm:monas", {founded:"1975", extract:"The National Monument, Monas, is a marble obelisk in the center of Jakarta, finished in 1975 with a gold-leaf flame at the top. It stands in a large park that used to be the ceremonial square of the colonial city. From the air it is a thin white needle in a dense, low metropolis, with the Istiqlal Mosque and the cathedral close by. An elevator runs to a viewing deck under the flame.", fact:"The flame is covered in gold leaf, not solid gold."});
  set("lm:nazca", {founded:"c. 500 BCE", extract:"The Nazca Lines are geoglyphs on the desert pampa of southern Peru, figures and lines made by moving dark stones to show the lighter ground underneath. The hummingbird, spider, and monkey are among the famous ones. They are best seen from the air, which is why a small strip at Nazca exists. From above they are pale drawings on a huge, empty terrace between the Andes and the sea. Who made them, and why, is still argued.", fact:"The figures survive because the pampa almost never sees rain."});
  set("lm:sigiriya", {founded:"477", extract:"Sigiriya is a granite rock fortress in central Sri Lanka, used as a palace in the fifth century under King Kashyapa. A stair and galleries climb the cliff; frescoes of women, the Mirror Wall, and lion-paw terraces remain. From the air the rock is a sudden plug of stone in a green plain, with gardens and moats laid out at its foot. The summit is wind and a view of jungle.", fact:"The name is often explained as lion rock, from the paws that still mark the final stair."});
  set("lm:taospueblo", {founded:"c. 1000", extract:"Taos Pueblo is a living adobe village north of Taos, New Mexico, with multi-story houses of earth and timber that have been occupied for centuries. Red Willow Creek runs through the plaza. The present church replaced one destroyed in 1847. From the air the pueblo is a tight cluster of brown walls at the foot of the Sangre de Cristo, still a community rather than a reconstruction. Photography rules belong to the pueblo.", fact:"It is one of the oldest continuously inhabited communities in the United States."});
  set("lm:unionstation", {founded:"1914", extract:"Denver Union Station is a Beaux-Arts terminal on Wynkoop Street, rebuilt after a 1894 fire and opened in its present form in 1914. The neon sign still reads Union Station Travel by Train. A 2014 renovation put a hotel and a train hall back into daily use, with commuter rail to the airport. From the air it is a long, low roof at the edge of downtown, with the Front Range west of the tracks. Snow on the stone is a local postcard.", fact:"The current hall sits on the site of Denver’s original 1881 union depot."});
})();
